  "method": "each upper arm's total humerothoracic elevation (the TT goal's elevation, or the measured A-pose elevation when the document omits the arm) drives its girdle bone: elevation (abduction) to 11 degrees and retraction (negative flexion) to 16 degrees at 180, both capped by the girdle's pinned range, from a zero knot at the A-pose rest elevation (Ludewig 2009; Inman 1944); the humeral goal is solved after the girdle moves, so the coupling does not add to the authored total. The pelvifemoral rhythm tilts the pelvis posteriorly by 18.1% of the larger trunk-relative thigh flexion (Murray 2002) about the hip centres, the lumbar joint reading the tilt as flexion",
